risk:
- An agent can now make the shared browser move. A run uses the mock provider only (proc.rs:30-36, 84-93), but the browser service it drives is real and loads real pages. What bounds it:
  - one holder at a time (state.rs:297-301);
  - 15 minutes per running child;
  - the hourly and daily counts per chat. These are a rate, not a bound (the rate step: pruning at KEPT is across chats, and run folders are never removed);
  - the agent's own graph list, --agent-run-graphs: wiki-hop by default, and never widened by --run-graphs.
  A parked run has no clock (R1a). An agent that starts a run and leaves it parked holds the browser until the person answers or abandons it. The page shows it in that chat's strip (GP-a), and other agents get a 409 that names nobody.
- Out-of-sight hosts (D9) only through --agent-run-graphs. The default, wiki-hop, opens only en.wikipedia.org (wiki-hop.json:66-69). An operator who names triage-linux or triage-botforge there lets a model pick args.ssh and reach another host unattended. That goes through eidolon::exec, which the guard rows never see (host.rs:836-861; rows.rn:5-10). The flag's help says so, and the perch does not second-guess the operator (the user question).
- Model text reaches eidolon as the graph's args. That was already true for the page's start. The args go on stdin (`--args -`, proc.rs:111-125), never argv, and are capped at 2 KiB for an agent. A run's journal and report are written from those args and pages, so read hands report and question back clipped, and PR's descriptions call them untrusted.
- A fourth secret file in ~/.config/eidolon. It is minted, rotated, read back and removed by the same code as term's (token.rs:67-135, 203-217), with no new path. A runs token is good only for /hub/runs/a/*, from a door's own socket.
- What an agent is told about other chats is shaped, not isolated. The measures:
  - the identical 404 sentence for another chat's run and a missing one;
  - the 409 that names no holder for another chat;
  - agent rows without chat;
  - no eidolon text (why, refused, out, err) in any agent answer.
  These steer the model and keep other chats' ids and eidolon's hints out of its context. They protect nothing from a same-uid process. A door's bash runs as the perch's user, and the guard rows refuse only aoide, ssh-like programs, the key words and minerva/hoot keys, not cat or a loopback curl (rows.rn:444-476). So it can read:
  - runs.json, 0600 to that same uid (state.rs:31), with every chat's tags, why, refused and session paths;
  - the run folders;
  - the master token file. With that it can curl the page's routes: tag any chat, answer or abandon a park, read any detail.
  AG's own risk says the same of the gate (AG.json: "a guard rail, not a boundary"). The security review checks that the hiding is applied on every agent answer, and it does NOT certify it as a boundary. A GW-style deny row for an agent reading <home>/runs* or the perch's token file is a follow-up, and the operator's call (notes).
- The refactor of RT's create. A regression in the page's start is what tests/runs.rs (unchanged) would catch, byte for byte.
- The same agent can start runs that its own workflow's tools could not. A run's own tool calls come from a workflow session, which attribution refuses (attrib.rs:18-21), so runs cannot start runs.
- Merge order. RT first: RA rebuilds RT's create. Then RA. The others are not code dependencies; whichever lands second rebases onto RA's anchors:
  - T2 (wf_0cb668b4-3e3-1, in flight) does not touch perch/src/agent/. Its diff is term/agent.rs, tests/term_agent.rs, tests/agent.rs:675 and its tests/README.md row; RA's only overlap is a neighbouring README row.
  - R2c (held) moves lib.rs's Options fields, main.rs's flag block and its flag test, and the Options literals in serve.rs, mesh/mod.rs and tests/mesh.rs.
  - M2 (held) edits tests/mesh.rs.
